<description>
# Rotary switch: the lights of a microscope

A demonstration of the Rotary Switch part ([ROTARY_SWITCH.md](../../docs/ROTARY_SWITCH.md)), Switches in the component list. Source: a circuit given in words, the lights of an analogue microscope (`electronics/microscope/specs/SPEC.md` §3.2–3.3): 12 V, a 4-position knob for two LEDs (off, LED 1, LED 2, both), each LED with its own series resistor.

## Parts and values

| Part | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `BAT` | 12 V | Chosen: the lights run on 12 V from a battery or an adapter. |
| `SW` | Rotary Switch, 2 poles, 4 positions | Chosen: pole A feeds LED 1 (its throws A2 and A4 are wired together), pole B feeds LED 2 (B3 and B4); position 1 connects nothing. A1, A3, B1 and B2 are open on purpose. |
| `R1`, `R2` | 330 Ω | Chosen: (12 − 3.2) V / 330 Ω = 26.7 mA per LED, within the LED's recommended 30 mA. |
| `LED1`, `LED2` | White, `Threshold` 3 V, `Resistance` 7 Ω | Chosen: a stand-in for the listing's LED (3.0 to 3.4 V, 30 mA recommended). |

## How to use it

Run: LED 1 lights (position 2). Drag the knob, click it (one detent clockwise), turn the wheel over it, or `ui dial SW &lt;0..3&gt;`:

| Position | LED 1 | LED 2 |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | off | off |
| 2 | 26.7 mA | off |
| 3 | off | 26.7 mA |
| 4 | 26.7 mA | 26.7 mA |

Expected: (12 − 3.2) / 330 = 26.7 mA; simulated [SIMULATED]: 26.70 mA in each case (the current through R1 and R2).

## Warnings left

None from the layout check (0 errors, 0 warnings). The circuit tool's fidelity report notes `LED2` as "drawn mirrored against the source": that reads the placement hints of the netlist, not the circuit (polarity OK: each anode is on the side of its resistor).

## create-circuit skill

- Version: 1.30.0 (commit 0f4f59f)
- Circuit: rotary_switch_lights.sim1

### Limitations found while building it

None reported.
</description>


